{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## The defect\n\n`scripts/check_config_reads.py` (the 6754 gate, wired as the \"Config-read gate\" step at\n`.github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml:506-509`) is meant to fail CI when a `_config_schema.py`\ndataclass field has no read site unless it carries a `# read-via:` marker. It does not do that.\n\n`_collect_reads` (script lines ~84-101) builds a GLOBAL SET of attribute NAMES read anywhere under\n`src/rebar`, then the check is effectively `field_name in reads` -- NOT `Class.field`.\n\nSo any field whose bare name is read on ANY other object anywhere in the tree passes the gate,\nregardless of whether that field itself is ever read.\n\n## Confirmed instance (historical -- the field has since been removed)\n\n`CodeHealthConfig.enabled` had ZERO read sites, yet passed the gate and carried no `# read-via:`\nmarker, because `.enabled` is read on unrelated configs (`UiConfig.enabled` at\n`_config_schema.py:416`, `McpConfig.auth_enabled`, `EnsureConfig.hint_enabled`, and several under\n`llm/`). Contrast the also-removed `CodeHealthConfig.analyzers`, whose bare name was read NOWHERE in\nthe tree, so it DID trip the gate and needed an explicit `# read-via: inert pending bug\ndce2-b93d-4112-451c` marker to keep CI green.\n\nThat asymmetry is the tell: the gate catches an inert field only when its name happens to be\nglobally unique. That is luck, not a check.\n\n**Re-baselined 2026-08-05.** `CodeHealthConfig.enabled` was REMOVED under `a573-00ea-2bf6-4eb1`\n(Gerrit change 1422), which merged into `origin/main` at `859b6797d9`; `CodeHealthConfig` now\ndeclares only `scan_roots` / `size_cap` / `size_near_fraction` (`_config_schema.py:534-539`). So the\nflagship instance is no longer reproducible on the live tree and is recorded here as history. It is\nNOT an edit target for this ticket, and no marker is placed on it. The defect itself is unchanged\nand is reproduced instead by a synthetic fixture (see the RED-first AC below), which is the more\ndurable proof anyway because it does not depend on any particular field surviving in the schema.\n\n## Why this matters beyond one field\n\nThis is a FALSE-NEGATIVE class, not a single miss. Common field names -- `enabled`, `path`,\n`timeout`, `remote`, `branch`, `mode` -- are exactly the ones most likely to be reused across\nconfigs and therefore exactly the ones this gate cannot police. The inert-config family it exists\nto prevent (281f, 9d7a, dce2, a573) is precisely the family it will keep missing.\n\n## SCOPE (operator ruling, 2026-08-05) -- fix the gate, REPORT what it flags, do NOT triage\n\nThe operator has ruled on scope. It is recorded here in the DESCRIPTION (not only in a comment)\nbecause the plan reviewer reads the description:\n\n**In scope.** Fix the name-resolution defect so a read is resolved to its OWNING dataclass rather\nthan matched as a bare attribute name anywhere under `src/rebar`. Then run the fixed gate across\nthe whole schema and REPORT the newly-flagged fields.\n\n**Explicitly OUT of scope: triaging the newly-flagged fields.** Each newly-flagged field is a\nwire-it-or-delete-it PRODUCT judgement -- the same call the operator made individually for\n`dce2-b93d-4112-451c` (delete) and `a573-00ea-2bf6-4eb1` (delete). Rationale: an agent must not make\nthose calls unsupervised inside a gate fix. Deleting or wiring a config field changes user-visible\nbehaviour, and that decision belongs to the operator, not to a side effect of a checker correction.\nThis ticket reports the list and stops.\n\n**Sequencing (the practical consequence).** A stricter gate can fail CI on the fields it newly\nflags, so the fix cannot land bare. The operator sanctioned two mechanisms: (a) land the fix with\naccurate `# read-via:` markers -- factual pointers for fields that ARE read through indirection, and\na tracking-ticket pointer for any field that is genuinely inert -- which is the mechanism the\ncodebase already uses (`TrackerConfig.branch:529`, `ReconcilerConfig.lock_lease_secs:431`, the\n`VerifyConfig` gate-key trio); or (b) land the fix behind resolution of those fields.\n\n**THIS TICKET CHOOSES (a).** Reason: (b) blocks a correct checker behind product decisions that are\nnot this ticket's to make, and leaves the false-negative class live meanwhile. Under (a) every\nmarker must be ACCURATE: it cites the real reader, or names a real open tracking ticket. Silently\nexempting a field to make CI green is forbidden.\n\n**Measured result of the sweep (prototype run on `859b6797d9`).** All four newly-flagged fields turn\nout to be LIVE and read through documented indirection, so NO wire-it-or-delete-it judgement arises\nand nothing needs operator triage:\n\n| Field | Why the old gate passed it | Real read site |\n|---|---|---|\n| `McpConfig.readonly` | `.readonly` also read as `ctx.readonly()` in `_mcp_reads.py:39` | `config.py:423` `mcp_readonly()` -- inside the excluded plumbing file |\n| `McpConfig.allow_llm` | `.allow_llm` also read as `ctx.allow_llm` in `_mcp_writes.py:42` | `mcp_server.py:431` `_mcp_gate(\"allow_llm\", ...)` -- STRING key |\n| `McpConfig.allow_jira_sync` | `.allow_jira_sync` also read as `ctx.allow_jira_sync` in `_mcp_reads.py:39` | `mcp_server.py:435` `_mcp_gate(\"allow_jira_sync\", ...)` -- STRING key |\n| `JiraConfig.allow_insecure` | `ReconcilerConfig.allow_insecure` is read at `adapters/jira_datacenter/settings.py:121` | `_config_schema.py` `JiraConfig.__post_init__` -- inside the excluded plumbing file |\n\nEach therefore receives a FACTUAL `# read-via:` marker naming that reader -- the same shape as the\nexisting `TrackerConfig.branch` marker. This is documentation of a verified read site, not a product\njudgement, so it stays inside the operator's ruling. If a future run flags a field with no such\nreader, it is reported and left for the operator, never silently marked.\n\n## Required negative fixtures (the gate must NOT false-fire on these)\n\nA correct owning-dataclass resolver still has to tolerate legitimate STRING-keyed reads. A prior\nmanual sweep of all config dataclass fields (under a573) surfaced these live-but-string-read cases;\nthey are required negative fixtures:\n\n- `ReconcilerConfig.lock_lease_secs:431` -- read via `getattr` in\n  `_engine/rebar_reconciler/_advisory_lock.py`; already carries a `# read-via:` marker.\n- `VerifyConfig.require_plan_review_for_close:163` (and its `require_plan_review_for_claim:172` /\n  `require_completion_verification_for_close:159` siblings) -- read via\n  `gate_enabled(root, \"<key>\", ...)` string keys in `_commands/gates.py`; already carry markers.\n\nThe fixed gate must leave these passing.\n\n## Design of the fix\n\nReplace the global name set with a per-read owner resolution in `scripts/check_config_reads.py`:\n\n1. Parse `_config_schema.py` into `class -> {field -> annotation}` and derive the SECTION map from\n   the root `Config` dataclass (`code_health -> CodeHealthConfig`, etc.).\n2. For every `*.py` under `src/rebar` (keeping the existing `_config_schema.py` / `config.py`\n   plumbing exclusion), resolve the RECEIVER expression of each `ast.Attribute` read to a set of\n   candidate owning schema classes, using: the section-attribute name (`x.code_health` ->\n   `CodeHealthConfig`), parameter/variable annotations naming a schema class, local assignment\n   propagation (`m = cfg.mcp; m.readonly`), function RETURN annotations (`load_config() -> Config`),\n   `IfExp` / `BoolOp` branches, and a cross-module fixed point that binds unannotated PARAMETERS\n   from their call sites (`build_composite_verifier(mcp_cfg)` called with `cfg.mcp`).\n3. A field is satisfied only by a read whose resolved owner set contains its declaring class.\n4. After the sweep, also report any existing `# read-via:` marker whose pointer names a rebar ticket\n   id, so a marker parked against a since-CLOSED ticket surfaces instead of silently persisting.\n   (Verified on `859b6797d9`: zero markers currently name a ticket id -- the dce2 one left with\n   `analyzers` -- so this step reports an empty list today and exists to catch the next one.)\n\n**Failure direction, stated honestly.** An unresolvable receiver contributes to NO field, so a live\nfield read only through a receiver the resolver cannot bind is newly FLAGGED -- this errs FAIL-LOUD,\nnot fail-quiet, and a false positive here would break CI. That risk is retired empirically rather\nthan by assertion: the prototype was run over the whole tree at `859b6797d9` and every one of the\nfour newly-flagged fields was traced by hand to a real reader (table above), with no live field left\nmis-flagged. The residual permissiveness is elsewhere and is fail-quiet: function names are keyed\nbare (module-agnostic), so a same-named function in another module can only WIDEN a binding.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The gate resolves a read to the OWNING dataclass, not a bare attribute name -- a field is\n      satisfied only by a read of that class's instance, so a same-named attribute on an unrelated\n      config no longer satisfies it.\n- [ ] A RED-first test proves the OLD gate passes a synthetic inert field whose name is reused on an\n      unrelated config, and that the fixed gate fails it. Run both versions in-process from a\n      snapshot COPY of the pre-fix script -- never `git stash`, which is global across all worktrees\n      sharing this host's git common dir.\n- [ ] The existing gate tests are MIGRATED to the owner-resolution contract rather than worked\n      around: `test_unrelated_same_named_attribute_keeps_the_gate_quiet` in\n      `tests/scripts/test_check_config_reads_heldout.py` currently PINS the defect as an \"accepted\n      trade-off\" and is inverted to expect a firing (with the docstring rewritten to say why); the\n      synthetic schemas `_SCHEMA_ONE_FIELD` / `_SCHEMA` gain a root `Config` dataclass + section\n      field so `cfg.mcp.ghost_knob` resolves; and each receiver shape in\n      `test_every_real_receiver_shape_counts_as_a_read` is kept passing by making its receiver\n      resolvable (annotation, section name, alias assignment, or a call site) rather than by\n      loosening the resolver. Weakening the resolver to keep the old pins green is explicitly\n      forbidden.\n- [ ] The two required negative fixtures above are pinned as tests over the REAL schema and still\n      pass.\n- [ ] The gate is re-run across the whole existing `_config_schema.py` and every newly-flagged field\n      is REPORTED (in a ticket comment and in the Gerrit change description). Per the operator\n      ruling they are NOT triaged here.\n- [ ] Each newly-flagged field gets an ACCURATE `# read-via:` marker citing its real reader (the\n      four in the table above), so the fix lands CI-green without a silent exemption. A field with\n      no reader would instead be reported and left for the operator.\n- [ ] Any `# read-via:` marker whose pointer names a rebar ticket id is reported (design step 4);\n      today that list is empty.\n- [ ] The real tree passes the fixed gate (`python scripts/check_config_reads.py` exits 0), and\n      `make lint` / `make typecheck` / the `tests/scripts` suite pass.\n\n## Provenance\n\nFound by a code-trace archaeology pass over the metrics/code-health system while scoping\ndce2-b93d-4112-451c. The gate's own escape hatch for `analyzers` was self-referential -- the bug id\nin the marker is what kept CI green -- which is legitimate, but it masked that the gate could not\nhave caught the neighbouring field at all."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785915449474145001,"uuid":"23346525-6918-4d1c-aa8c-879b9396b1c2"}